1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,420
You can learn every DevOps tool,

2
00:00:02,550 --> 00:00:04,641
and still not know how they fit together.

3
00:00:05,191 --> 00:00:07,111
That is where most beginners get stuck.

4
00:00:08,061 --> 00:00:10,279
This bootcamp puts every tool in order.

5
00:00:11,129 --> 00:00:13,732
Welcome to the DevOps Engineering Bootcamp.

6
00:00:14,282 --> 00:00:19,189
A step by step course, from zero to building, securing, and running real systems.

7
00:00:20,739 --> 00:00:23,491
Here is how we teach every tool. Five steps.

8
00:00:24,041 --> 00:00:27,028
Learn it: understand the idea, and why it exists.

9
00:00:27,578 --> 00:00:30,116
Build it: set it up yourself, in a working lab.

10
00:00:30,666 --> 00:00:33,013
Break it: watch what fails, in a safe lab.

11
00:00:33,563 --> 00:00:35,760
Fix it: find the cause, and repair it.

12
00:00:36,310 --> 00:00:39,361
Operate it: secure it, monitor it, and keep it running.

13
00:00:42,411 --> 00:00:44,459
The course is planned in nine stages,

14
00:00:44,589 --> 00:00:46,808
and each stage builds on the one before.

15
00:00:47,358 --> 00:00:48,296
Foundations:

16
00:00:48,846 --> 00:00:49,657
Linux,

17
00:00:49,787 --> 00:00:50,555
Bash,

18
00:00:50,685 --> 00:00:51,453
Python,

19
00:00:51,583 --> 00:00:52,372
and Git.

20
00:00:52,922 --> 00:00:54,672
Applications and build tools:

21
00:00:55,222 --> 00:00:55,990
Java,

22
00:00:56,120 --> 00:00:56,866
Maven,

23
00:00:56,996 --> 00:00:57,786
Tomcat,

24
00:00:57,916 --> 00:00:59,025
and databases.

25
00:00:59,575 --> 00:01:00,834
Then CI/CD:

26
00:01:01,384 --> 00:01:02,258
Jenkins,

27
00:01:02,388 --> 00:01:03,455
SonarQube,

28
00:01:03,585 --> 00:01:05,142
and containers with Docker.

29
00:01:05,692 --> 00:01:06,674
Then the cloud,

30
00:01:06,804 --> 00:01:07,849
AWS,

31
00:01:07,979 --> 00:01:08,832
and Azure,

32
00:01:08,962 --> 00:01:09,901
Terraform,

33
00:01:10,031 --> 00:01:10,842
Ansible,

34
00:01:10,972 --> 00:01:12,038
and Kubernetes,

35
00:01:12,168 --> 00:01:13,832
with Helm and Argo CD.

36
00:01:14,382 --> 00:01:17,113
Then security,

37
00:01:17,243 --> 00:01:19,120
monitoring, and reliability,

38
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:21,021
and a final capstone project.

39
00:01:21,571 --> 00:01:25,518
This is the plan. Lessons are published stage by stage, in order.

40
00:01:28,568 --> 00:01:30,296
Now, how the program flows.

41
00:01:31,046 --> 00:01:33,094
The course is planned in nine stages,

42
00:01:33,224 --> 00:01:35,421
with forty eight masterclasses in total.

43
00:01:35,971 --> 00:01:40,281
A masterclass covers one tool, or one topic, such as Git, or Docker.

44
00:01:40,831 --> 00:01:42,857
Each masterclass has five levels:

45
00:01:43,607 --> 00:01:44,397
Beginner,

46
00:01:44,527 --> 00:01:45,508
Intermediate,

47
00:01:45,638 --> 00:01:46,513
Advanced,

48
00:01:46,643 --> 00:01:47,496
Production,

49
00:01:47,626 --> 00:01:48,607
and Mastery.

50
00:01:49,757 --> 00:01:53,043
Each level is a few short lessons, about five minutes each.

51
00:01:53,593 --> 00:01:56,899
And each level ends with a project, that you build yourself.

52
00:01:57,449 --> 00:02:00,777
At Mastery, you get only the requirements, and no steps.

53
00:02:01,327 --> 00:02:05,551
Every stage ends with an exam, on a scenario you have not seen before.

54
00:02:08,101 --> 00:02:10,640
One project follows you through the whole course.

55
00:02:11,190 --> 00:02:13,558
It is called CloudCart, an online shop.

56
00:02:14,408 --> 00:02:16,733
First, a simple Java application,

57
00:02:16,863 --> 00:02:20,255
running on Tomcat, with a PostgreSQL database.

58
00:02:21,105 --> 00:02:22,897
Then we automate it with Jenkins,

59
00:02:23,027 --> 00:02:25,075
and check its quality with SonarQube.

60
00:02:25,625 --> 00:02:27,950
Then we put it in a container, with Docker.

61
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:30,762
Then we create its servers with Terraform.

62
00:02:31,312 --> 00:02:33,616
Then we run it on Kubernetes, with Helm.

63
00:02:34,166 --> 00:02:37,920
And finally, we add monitoring, with Prometheus and Grafana.

64
00:02:39,070 --> 00:02:42,313
By the end, you have built, secured, and run a real system.

65
00:02:45,063 --> 00:02:46,962
You do not have to start at the beginning.

66
00:02:47,512 --> 00:02:48,621
Complete beginner:

67
00:02:49,171 --> 00:02:51,496
start at lesson one, and follow the order.

68
00:02:52,446 --> 00:02:54,089
Experienced administrator:

69
00:02:54,639 --> 00:02:57,028
pass a placement check, and skip the basics.

70
00:02:57,978 --> 00:02:59,834
Developer moving into DevOps:

71
00:03:00,384 --> 00:03:05,504
skip the application topics, and focus on CI/CD, containers, and the cloud.

72
00:03:06,454 --> 00:03:10,444
Every skip is earned, by passing that masterclass's final challenge.

73
00:03:13,194 --> 00:03:15,221
Every lesson follows the same shape.

74
00:03:15,771 --> 00:03:16,709
First, why:

75
00:03:17,259 --> 00:03:18,817
the problem the tool solves.

76
00:03:19,367 --> 00:03:21,329
Then the concept, in plain words.

77
00:03:21,879 --> 00:03:23,245
Then a demonstration,

78
00:03:23,375 --> 00:03:25,572
with real commands, and real output.

79
00:03:26,122 --> 00:03:27,445
No made up results.

80
00:03:27,995 --> 00:03:28,997
Then practice,

81
00:03:29,127 --> 00:03:30,322
which you do yourself,

82
00:03:30,452 --> 00:03:32,671
and a checker that tells you if you got it right.

83
00:03:33,221 --> 00:03:36,038
Every lesson also comes with a kit: notes,

84
00:03:36,038 --> 00:03:37,380
a practice exercise,

85
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:38,117
a solution,

86
00:03:38,117 --> 00:03:39,727
and a command reference.

87
00:03:41,077 --> 00:03:44,171
Each Masterclass ends with five hands-on projects.

88
00:03:46,721 --> 00:03:48,065
What do you need to start?

89
00:03:48,615 --> 00:03:51,602
A computer that can run a virtual machine, or Docker.

90
00:03:52,152 --> 00:03:54,968
We recommend eight gigabytes of memory, or more.

91
00:03:55,918 --> 00:03:58,798
A free GitHub account, from the Git lessons onward.

92
00:03:59,348 --> 00:04:02,334
A steady pace: one or two lessons a day works well.

93
00:04:02,884 --> 00:04:07,578
And the habit of practising. Watching alone will not make you a DevOps engineer.

94
00:04:10,128 --> 00:04:12,325
A few promises about what to expect.

95
00:04:12,875 --> 00:04:14,347
No experience needed.

96
00:04:14,897 --> 00:04:18,439
We explain every new term and command the first time it appears.

97
00:04:18,989 --> 00:04:20,759
Linux lessons use Ubuntu,

98
00:04:20,889 --> 00:04:24,772
a free system you can run on your own computer, in a virtual machine.

99
00:04:25,322 --> 00:04:29,973
When something is an illustration, and not a real capture, we label it on screen.

100
00:04:30,523 --> 00:04:33,979
Some cloud lessons may need an account with a cloud provider.

101
00:04:34,529 --> 00:04:36,854
We will tell you the cost before you click anything.

102
00:04:37,404 --> 00:04:39,025
We cannot promise you a job.

103
00:04:39,575 --> 00:04:43,223
We can promise real practice, and a way to check your own progress.

104
00:04:45,773 --> 00:04:47,074
Start with lesson one:

105
00:04:47,624 --> 00:04:49,566
the software development life cycle.

106
00:04:50,116 --> 00:04:52,932
How does an idea become something customers use?

107
00:04:53,782 --> 00:04:56,192
Subscribe, and follow the playlist in order.

108
00:04:56,742 --> 00:04:58,065
See you in lesson one.
